\ Phase 4 slice 6a follow-on: generic array search/sort, comparator-driven
\ rather than bound-driven. There is no trait/impl mechanism connecting a
\ bound to a type's capability (`Ord`'s `is_ord` is `ty.is_numeric()` and
\ nothing else), so a hypothetical `Cmp` bound would only ever admit the same
\ numerics `Ord` already does -- worthless for comparing a user struct. Both
\ words below instead take an explicit three-way comparator quotation
\ (negative/zero/positive, `strcmp`/`qsort` convention), which is the only
\ mechanism that actually reaches a user type today.
\
\ `sort` is a plain bottom-up (non-adaptive) merge sort, not powersort: a
\ generic word's body cannot construct an array (`fill` is unreachable inside
\ a poly body, confirmed directly, literal count or not), so any scratch
\ memory a generic sort needs has to be a caller-supplied argument. Real
\ powersort needs two such buffers -- the merge scratch and a growable
\ run-descriptor stack for its power-based merge-order policy -- and a
\ three-array call site for an ordinary `sort` call was judged not worth it.

import: "combinators.sth" c | times | ;

export: bin_search bin_search-helper sort ;

\ Binary search over an array kept sorted by `cmp`. Converges to the
\ leftmost insertion point (`lower_bound`) using only `cmp`'s sign, then
\ checks equality once at the end -- so duplicates report their leftmost
\ index. The halving loop is `bin_search-helper`, a self-*tail* recursion on
\ the `lo`/`hi` pair in the same shape as `times-helper`: the recursive call
\ sits in tail position of an `if` arm, so it lowers to a loop back-edge (D8)
\ and runs in constant stack. It stops when the window closes, rather than
\ running a fixed 32 halvings and idling once converged.
\ `bin_search-helper` is exported for the same reason `times-helper` is: the
\ REPL's dlopen import path retains only a module's exported words.
: bin_search-helper inline ( ['T: Copy 'N] 'T ~[ 'T 'T -- i64 ] i64 i64 -- ['T 'N] i64 )
  | arr key cmp lo hi |
  lo hi lt 
  ~[
    lo hi add 1 shr | mid |
    &arr mid >usize &> @ key cmp call 0 lt 
    ~[ arr key cmp mid 1 add hi bin_search-helper ]
    ~[ arr key cmp lo mid bin_search-helper ]
    if
  ] 
  ~[ arr lo ] 
  if ;

: bin_search inline ( ['T: Copy 'N] 'T ~[ 'T 'T -- i64 ] -- ['T 'N] usize bool )
  | key cmp | 
  len >i64 
  | arr  n|
  arr key cmp 0 n bin_search-helper
  | sorted lo |
  lo n lt 
  ~[
    &sorted lo >usize &> @ key cmp call 0 eq
  ] 
  ~[
    false
  ] 
  if
  | found |
  sorted lo >usize found
;

: min2 ( i64 i64 -- i64 )
  over over lt ~[ drop ] ~[ swap drop ] if ;

\ In-place (caller-supplied scratch) bottom-up merge sort. `scratch` must be
\ the same length as `arr`; its contents on entry are irrelevant, and the
\ array returned in its place is the other one -- whichever of the two
\ physical buffers did *not* end up holding the sorted result.
\
\ The outer `times` row (`ca`/`cs`/`cw`: current source, current
\ destination, current run width) is reassigned to the fresh merge output
\ every active pass and passed through unchanged on an idle one, so whichever
\ value `ca` holds at the end already holds the answer -- no separate
\ "which physical buffer is which" flag is needed. Both the outer pass loop
\ and the inner per-block loop run a fixed 32/`n`-bounded `times` rather than
\ stopping early: an array's length is a `u32`, so `ceil(log2(n)) <= 32`
\ passes always suffice, and `n` block-start attempts always suffice to walk
\ every block, with the excess iterations idling as a no-op once `cw >= n`
\ (outer) or `j >= n` (inner).
: sort inline ( ['T: Copy 'N] ['T 'N] ~[ 'T 'T -- i64 ] -- ['T 'N] ['T 'N] )
  | cmp | | scratch |
  len >i64 | n | | arr |
  arr scratch 1 32 ~[ | i |
    | ca cs cw |
    cw n lt ~[
      0 n ~[ | k |
        | j |
        j n lt ~[
          j cw add n min2 | mid |
          j cw 2 mul add n min2 | hi |
          j mid hi j sub ~[ | step |
            | l r |
            l mid lt r hi lt and ~[
              &ca l >usize &> @ &ca r >usize &> @ cmp call 0 lt ~[
                &!cs j step add >usize &!> &ca l >usize &> @ !
                l 1 add r
              ] ~[
                &!cs j step add >usize &!> &ca r >usize &> @ !
                l r 1 add
              ] if
            ] ~[
              l mid lt ~[
                &!cs j step add >usize &!> &ca l >usize &> @ !
                l 1 add r
              ] ~[
                &!cs j step add >usize &!> &ca r >usize &> @ !
                l r 1 add
              ] if
            ] if
          ] times
          drop drop
          j cw 2 mul add
        ] ~[
          j
        ] if
      ] times
      drop
      cs ca cw 2 mul
    ] ~[
      ca cs cw
    ] if
  ] times
  | ra rs rw | rw drop
  ra rs
;
